<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kerch Strait</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cimmerian Bosporus, Strait of Kerch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch">Kerch</a> Strait, the Cimmerian Bosporus of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, links the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and the Taman peninsula. From the 6th century BCE Greek colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panticapaeum">Panticapaeum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanagoria">Phanagoria</a> lined its shores, and around 480 BCE they united as the Bosporan Kingdom. The ancient name recalls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, a people whom the Greeks believed had once lived there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerch-strait.jpg" alt="Kerch Strait" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerch_Strait,_Ukraine,_Russia,_near_natural_colors_satellite_image,_LandSat-5,_2011-08-30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
